          -e strict **types/lint** gates, and the Opus-tier tasks (`async-memo`, `expr-eval`) are where the 20-point headroom to Opus lives (per eval design; the per-task detail is in `charts/task_heatmap.svg`).
      100 +### (d) Capability vs haiku/Sonnet/Opus — the 27B is well below the ladder. (MEASURED)
      101 +Best local cell = **33% TS** (un-d64-f16-rb4096). Reference one-shots (calibration): **haiku 74%
      102 +(Δ−42) · Sonnet 85% (Δ−52) · Opus 95% (Δ−63)**. The local 27B lands **below even the haiku floor** on
      103 +this strict, depth-loaded eval, and **hard-passes nothing** (0/120). **INFERRED root cause** (corroborated
      104 +by the judge, below): the 27B routinely gets the *algorithm* right — the judge scores design/clarity ~4/5
      105 +and the deterministic `fails:` lists repeatedly show `edge` and `tests` failing while the *design* is sound —
      106 +but it cannot clear the **strict `tsc`+`eslint`** wall and the hidden edge suite one-shot. This is the
      107 +"TDD + linting bottleneck" the eval was built to expose (`eval-design.md` §5b), made measurable: capability
      108 +here is gated by strict-typing/lint/edge discipline, not by problem-solving. **Note on the reference bar:**
      109 +the reference tiers are one-shot *no-think* calibration on a different SUT — a deliberately generous floor —
      110 +so "below haiku" is a strong statement about strict one-shot code quality at depth, not a claim the 27B is
      111 +useless with iteration.
      112
